msgid "LOOP_HELP"
msgstr "A measure is a group of beats between vertical lines. From and Through include both end measures. Play selected section repeats them with your count-in setting."

msgid "HELP_STRINGS"
msgstr "1. Read tabs from left to right. The six lines represent guitar strings. The top line is string 1, the thinnest and highest sounding; the bottom is string 6, the thickest. Tabs assume standard tuning: E–A–D–G–B–E from thickest to thinnest. Use Menu → Tuner & listening, or a separate tuner."

msgid "HELP_FRETS"
msgstr "2. A fret is a metal strip across the guitar neck. A tab number tells you which fret to use, not which finger. For 3, press just behind the third fret toward the headstock, then pluck that string. A 0 means pluck without pressing. Vertically aligned numbers are played together: a chord."

msgid "HELP_STAFF"
msgstr "3. The five-line staff is the sheet music reference. Higher positions mean higher pitches; note shapes show duration. Here, a quarter note lasts one beat, a half note two, and an eighth note half a beat. A rest means silence. A tie joins same-pitch notes into one held sound. Guitar treble is written an octave higher than it sounds: the same note name in the next higher register."

msgid "HELP_TIMING"
msgstr "4. A beat is the pulse you count along with; a measure groups beats between vertical lines. In Playback, turn on Beat clicks for a steady pulse or lower the speed to give yourself more time. Tap the music to move playback there. The rhythm display is simplified; audio keeps the original timing."

msgid "HELP_TECHNIQUES"
msgstr "5. In About this arrangement (under Songs), choose Basic tab, Strum with a pick or Fingerpick. A strum bracket marks the strings to sweep; X means keep that string quiet. B marks a possible barre: one fretting finger presses several strings at one fret. For fingerpicking, T is thumb; I, M, R are index, middle, ring of the picking hand. Read the warnings: simplified tabs may omit notes that remain in the staff and sound."

msgid "FOLLOW_HINT"
